```apex
trigger OpportunityTrigger on Opportunity
{
    fflib_SObjectDomain.triggerHandler(Opportunities.class);
}
```

**Design notes**

- **Qualification rules:** `afterInsert` adds records already created as `Closed Won`; `afterUpdate` adds only those whose previous stage (`Trigger.oldMap`) was different. Editing an already-won opportunity therefore never re-qualifies, and no other stages qualify.
- **At most one service call per invocation:** qualifying Ids are accumulated into a `Set<Id>` and `startFulfilment` is called once at the end of each handler, and only when the set is non-empty — satisfying "not at all when none qualify".
- **Bulk safety:** every record in the current trigger batch is evaluated in the loop, so up to 200 qualifying opportunities are sent in a single call. Separate batches each get their own (single) call, consistent with the per-invocation contract.
- **Testability / fflib conventions:** the service is obtained from `Application.Service.newInstance(...)`, so tests can swap in a mock via `Application.Service.setMock(IOpportunityFulfilmentService.class, mock)`. The trigger contains only the `triggerHandler` call, and the domain exposes the standard `Constructor` inner class implementing `fflib_SObjectDomain.IConstructable`.
- **After-update/after-insert context:** `startFulfilment` is invoked in `after` events only, so the opportunity Ids are committed and the downstream service (which registers `Task` records via the Unit of Work) can safely reference them.

