                <span>{task.label}</span>
            </li>
        </template>
    </ul>
    <p class="remaining">{remaining} remaining</p>
    <lightning-input class="new-task" label="New task" value={newLabel} onchange={handleLabelChange}></lightning-input>
    <lightning-button class="add" label="Add" onclick={handleAdd}></lightning-button>
</template>
```

Summary of the fixes:

1. **Heading**: no longer mutated in `connectedCallback` (which destroyed the original value and ran only once, so later changes were ignored). A public getter/setter stores the original value; a `headingUpper` getter renders the upper-cased display copy.
2. **Tasks copying**: a `tasks` setter replaces `items` with a shallow copy of the array and each task object, so the parent's data is never mutated and a new array from the parent replaces the list (even if set after connect).
3. **Reactivity for add/toggle**: arrays are replaced (`[...this.items, task]`, `this.items.map(...)`) and toggled tasks are replaced with new objects instead of being mutated in place — this is what makes the new row render and the `remaining` counter update.
4. **Toggle crash**: `dataset.id` is a string, so the lookup now coerces `task.id` with `String(...)`, preventing `undefined.done` errors on numeric ids.
5. **Add handler**: the label is trimmed (whitespace-only input is ignored with no event and no clearing side effects), the dispatched `detail.label` now contains the label actually added (previously it dispatched the already-cleared `newLabel`, i.e. an empty string), and the event type is the lowercase `taskadded` so parent `ontaskadded` listeners match.

